 "readings": [],
 "notes": "ESP32-S3 version of the BLE IMU + magnetometer tag (derived from the photo reconstruction). Verify before building.\nRadio: the ESP32-S3-WROOM-1 module brings its own crystal, flash, RF matching and PCB antenna — the nRF52832 crystals, DC/DC inductors, DEC capacitors, antenna match and SWD pads are gone. Programming and serial are over native USB (IO19/IO20) through the USB-C port, protected by U3 (USBLC6-2).\nPower: TP4054 charges the cell at 100 mA. D1 + Q1 share the load: with USB plugged the system runs from VBUS through D1 and Q1 isolates the battery; unplugged, Q1 turns on (its gate is pulled down by the VBUS-sense divider) and the cell feeds VSYS. AP2112K-3.3 (600 mA) replaces the 250 mA MCP1700 because Wi-Fi transmit bursts reach ≈ 350 mA; 22 µF + 100 nF sit at the module.\nRemoved as unnecessary: CHG and button pull-ups (use the ESP32 internal pull-ups), the 10 µF on the IMU, the reset pull-up (replaced by the EN RC), duplicated sensor caps (VDD and VDDIO share one 100 nF per sensor).\nAdded: EN reset RC (10 kΩ + 1 µF) with a RESET button, BOOT button on IO0, status LED on IO16, USB-present sense (100 k/100 k on IO2), battery sense moved behind the power path on IO1 (ADC1, works with Wi-Fi on), USB ESD protection, 10 µF on VBUS.\nGPIO map: SDA IO8, SCL IO9, IMU INT1/INT2 IO4/IO5, MAG INT IO6, button IO7, CHG IO15, LED IO16, VBAT ADC IO1, VBUS sense IO2. Strapping pins IO0, IO3, IO45, IO46 are left free (IO0 only on the BOOT button).\nBattery: the ESP32-S3 averages ≈ 80 mA with Wi-Fi and ≈ 30–60 mA with BLE only, so a 100 mAh cell lasts about 1–3 h awake; use deep sleep (≈ 8 µA) and IMU wake-on-motion interrupts for long life. See Calculators → Power budget.\nToggle S_USB in the schematic (simulation only) to watch the power path switch between USB and battery.",
